# Day 3 — Responsive CSS

## 1. Media Queries
Media queries are used to make a website look good on different screen sizes.

```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  /* CSS for mobile */
}
```

- `max-width: 768px` means the styles apply when the screen is 768px or smaller.
- This is a common breakpoint for mobile layouts.

## 2. Changing Flex Direction
```css
header {
  flex-direction: column;
}
```

On mobile, header items stack vertically instead of staying in a row.

## 3. Transition
```css
transition: color 0.3s ease;
```

A transition makes hover effects smooth instead of sudden.

- `0.3s` = duration
- `ease` = speed style

## 4. Important Responsive Ideas
- Always test using browser DevTools and mobile view.
- Use `flex-direction: column` for mobile layouts.
- Reduce padding and margin on small screens.
- Keep text readable on all devices.

## 5. Best Practice
Write desktop CSS first, then add media queries for mobile.

## Core Formula So Far
- HTML → Structure
- CSS → Design
- Flexbox → Alignment
- Media Query → Responsive
